#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""nav-deck: the flight instrument for the command deck. No AI.

comms-deck carries the conversation; this screen carries the map. It reads
the deck's own public surfaces — `comms-deck status --json`, the note log
(log.jsonl), the activity log (activity.jsonl), the per-seat usage files
the statuslines save, and Hyprland's `clients`/`workspaces` — and draws
one full-screen picture:

  SEATS        the ready order, with each seat's note load and spent countdown
  METERS       each seat's plan usage (5-hour, weekly, monthly, context)
  WARP CORE    the cockpit in the middle: a first-person starfield whose
               speed is the deck's own traffic (notes this hour, busy
               seats, load), with each star burning in its seat's colour,
               doled out by the context split, and the reticle — the sight
               at the vanishing point — sits on the screen's centre, with
               seats with waiting notes plotted as contacts (their first
               letter, at a bearing of their own); WARP/SHLD readouts
  WORKSPACES   which windows sit on which workspace, including special:magic
  VITALS       memory left, a sparkline of the recent past, the swap warning
  DECK PULSE   notes per hour today and who carried them

It is a viewer: it never sends a note and never types into a window. Its
one hand-on-the-deck move is `w <seat>`, which calls `comms-deck wake` and
lets that program's own rules (never wake a spent agent) decide.

It was born the same morning as this line in the kernel's OOM dump:

    Free swap = 0kB, Total swap = 0kB

so VITALS watches memory above everything else and keeps a short history
in ~/.local/share/comms-deck/navigation/ (one small file per day).

    nav-deck              live view (redraws about once a second)
    nav-deck --once       one frame to stdout and exit
    nav-deck --once --plain   no colours (for pipes and tests)
    nav-deck --self-test  render a fake home tree; assert; touch nothing real

On a wide screen the deck is drawn in the comms-deck look (claude's
framework, imported from the installed script, not copied): numbered
panels under a strip and over a ticker —

  01 CREW · 02 ACTIVITY   the left column, fed by comms-deck's own builders
  04 WARP CORE            the cockpit in the centre: the starfield and the
                          reticle, which still sits on the screen's exact
                          centre; 05 EVENT TAIL sits under it (the cockpit's
                          log: seat state flips and the newest notes)
  03 USAGE · 06 WORKSPACES · 07 VITALS · 08 NAV-TIME · 09 DECK PULSE
                          the right column (07 carries the stream status
                          row: obs/ffmpeg seen on the box)
  the ticker + keys       notes scroll along the bottom; c chat, n notes,
                          m mail, ` comms, 2 mail deck, 1 back here.

On a narrow screen (under the look's 150 columns) the deck keeps the old
plain panels, so nothing is lost on a small window.

Written by: vibe (2026-11-26), inspired by comms-deck by claude.
NAV-TIME panel by devin (2026-11-26). Warp-core seat colours by devin
(2026-11-26).
"""
